# Three files decide what every answer is allowed to contain. A change to any of them
# gets a second pair of eyes, always — not because the author is untrusted, but because
# a permission bug here is silent: it returns a plausible answer that is simply too wide.

/src/brain/core/entitlement.py   @rpsj2230
/src/brain/core/scope.py         @rpsj2230
/src/brain/gate/                 @rpsj2230

# The redactor and the policy it reads. The redactor decides, field by field, what
# survives into an answer; the policy is the table it decides from. A rule quietly
# reclassified from confidential to internal changes every answer that touches that
# field, and changes nothing that a test about redaction would notice.
/src/brain/core/redaction.py     @rpsj2230
/src/brain/core/field_policy.py  @rpsj2230

# Where a scope stops being Python and becomes SQL. This one is here because it has
# already gone wrong: an unescaped LIKE and a string splitting into an IN list both
# made the SQL admit rows the Python evaluator refused, so the two halves of one
# predicate disagreed and the wider half was the one that ran.
/src/brain/core/scope_sql.py     @rpsj2230

# The projection: what a caller is allowed to be shown before anybody asks a question.
/src/brain/core/projection.py    @rpsj2230

# The invariant suite is the only thing standing between a refactor and a leak.
# Weakening a test must be as deliberate as weakening the code it guards.
/tests/invariants/               @rpsj2230

# CI is where the sweeps and the deploy gate live.
/.github/workflows/              @rpsj2230
